Nested slots are a feature in Vue.js that allow you to create nested components within a parent component. This can be useful for creating complex layouts or reusable UI elements. Here's an example of how to use nested slots in Vue.js with TypeScript and async/await:
```typescript
import { Component, Prop } from 'vue';

@Component({
  template: `
    <div>
      <slot name="header"></slot>
      <slot></slot>
      <slot name="footer"></slot>
    </div>
  `,
})
export class MyComponent extends Vue {
  @Prop() header!: string;
  @Prop() footer!: string;
}

const app = new Vue({
  el: '#app',
  data: () => ({
    message: '',
  }),
});
```
In this example, we define a `MyComponent` component that has three slots: "header", "default" (which is the unnamed slot), and "footer". The `message` property is passed down to the child component as a prop using the `@Prop()` decorator.

To use the nested slots, we need to wrap our content inside the `<template>` tag of the parent component. We can then reference the slots by their names using the `v-slot` directive. For example:
```html
<my-component v-bind="{ header: 'Hello World!' }">
  <p>This is some text.</p>
  <button v-on:click="handleClick">Click me!</button>
</my-component>
```
In this case, the "header" slot will contain the text "Hello World!" and the default slot will contain the paragraph element and button. When the button is clicked, it will call the `handleClick` method on the parent component.

Here's an example GitHub repo that demonstrates how to use nested slots in Vue.js with TypeScript and async/await:

* https://github.com/vuejs/vue-async-example
